ttle, the whole batch fails. I bring that same habit to my work when I check data quality, map workflows, or build reports that leaders rely on.</p>", "para3_impact_story_html": "<p>I also believe analysis is only useful when it solves a real problem on the ground. At Concentrix, our hiring data showed that new joiners were struggling once they started work. Sending another dashboard to management was not fixing the issue. I looked closely at the hiring steps and worked with leadership to bring our training team directly into the interview stage. That simple change improved hire quality by 84 percent. In my current role at Highspring India, I use the same practical approach across four engineering teams to automate recurring reports and cut manual work by 40 percent.</p>", "para4_resilience_html": "<p>My day-to-day work covers SQL, Python, Tableau, and working closely with business and tech teams. If the team at Turing uses an internal tool I have not worked with yet, I learn new systems quickly. Practical challenges early in my career taught me how to stay resourceful, focus on the basics, and get things done without hand-holding. When I take ownership of a task, nobody has to follow up twice.</p>", "para5_closing_html": "<p>I would welcome the chance to bring this steady, practical approach to Turing. Thank you for your time and consideration, and I look forward to speaking with you soon.</p>"}, "index": 4, "role": "Technical Business Analyst", "category": "technical_business_analyst", "jobUrl": "https://careers.turing.com/roles", "jdSnippet": "About the Role\nTuring is seeking a Technical Business Analyst who can operate comfortably in two worlds at once: the day-to-day realities of Operations and Finance, and the practical constraints of building software. This is a role for someone who listens deeply, asks sharp questions, maps real workflows, and translates that understanding into clear, testable requirements that engineering teams can deliver with confidence.